{"reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting due to BERT dominance and ineffective fusion. Historical experiments show that simply concatenating features without learned weighting leads to instability, and progressive unfreezing helps balance training. The current execution shows validation loss spikes after epoch 4, confirming overfitting. Therefore, the most promising modifications are: 1) in model_design, replace simple concatenation with a cross-attention fusion module to dynamically weight features per sample; 2) in training_evaluation, implement progressive unfreezing and gradient surgery to ensure balanced gradient flow. These changes directly address the root cause without altering other successful components like random seed or data split.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of BERT [CLS], CNN, and MLP features in the classifier with a multi-head cross-attention fusion module. This module computes query from BERT features, key-value from CNN and MLP features, and combines them via attention, then adds a residual connection. A gating mechanism with sigmoid and a learned temperature parameter controls fusion strength. The classifier remains the same but takes the attended fused representation. Implementation: define a CrossAttentionFusion layer with 4 heads, hidden_dim=256; integrate it in the forward pass before the classifier. Inputs/outputs: forward method signature unchanged; fusion layer added as attribute.", "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-phase training schedule: Phase 1 (epochs 1-3) freezes BERT and trains only the custom heads and new fusion module with lr=2e-4; Phase 2 unfreezes BERT with lr=2e-5 and applies gradient clipping max_norm=0.5 and gradient normalization (scale gradients so that BERT and custom contributions have equal norm). Use early stopping patience increased to 7 to accommodate more careful training. Implementation: add a 'freeze_bert' flag, switch after 3 epochs; in optimizer step, use torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_ and a custom gradient_normalize function. Interface: same training loop structure, same dataloaders."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting due to BERT dominance and ineffective fusion. Historical experiments show that simply concatenating features without learned weighting leads to instability, and progressive unfreezing helps balance training. The current execution shows validation loss spikes after epoch 4, confirming overfitting. Therefore, the most promising modifications are: 1) in model_design, replace simple concatenation with a cross-attention fusion module to dynamically weight features per sample; 2) in training_evaluation, implement progressive unfreezing and gradient surgery to ensure balanced gradient flow. These changes directly address the root cause without altering other successful components like random seed or data split.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the simple concatenation of BERT [CLS], CNN, and MLP features in the classifier with a multi-head cross-attention fusion module. This module computes query from BERT features, key-value from CNN and MLP features, and combines them via attention, then adds a residual connection. A gating mechanism with sigmoid and a learned temperature parameter controls fusion strength. The classifier remains the same but takes the attended fused representation. Implementation: define a CrossAttentionFusion layer with 4 heads, hidden_dim=256; integrate it in the forward pass before the classifier. Inputs/outputs: forward method signature unchanged; fusion layer added as attribute.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-phase training schedule: Phase 1 (epochs 1-3) freezes BERT and trains only the custom heads and new fusion module with lr=2e-4; Phase 2 unfreezes BERT with lr=2e-5 and applies gradient clipping max_norm=0.5 and gradient normalization (scale gradients so that BERT and custom contributions have equal norm). Use early stopping patience increased to 7 to accommodate more careful training. Implementation: add a 'freeze_bert' flag, switch after 3 epochs; in optimizer step, use torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_ and a custom gradient_normalize function. Interface: same training loop structure, same dataloaders."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting as the root cause, suggesting freezing BERT and using a single-phase discriminative learning rate strategy. Historical experiments show that (1) successful approaches kept the transformer frozen and used differential learning rates, while (2) failed experiments that unfroze BERT led to instability and overfitting. Therefore, I focus on two modules: data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add input validation preventing NaN predictions (inspired by a successful historical fix), and model_design to simplify the architecture by removing the CNN branch (which can cause index errors) and freezing BERT entirely, as recommended by the initial plan and historical successes.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add input validation to check for NaN or infinite values in stylometric features and character IDs before feeding them into the model. This prevents gradient explosion and unstable predictions, as seen in a successful historical experiment that fixed NaN issues by reducing learning rates and adding clipping. Implementation: after extracting stylometric features, replace any NaN or inf values with 0; similarly, after tokenizing characters, ensure no out-of-range indices by clamping character IDs to [0, vocab_size-1] and setting padding token to 0. Interface: preserve existing function signatures and data structures.", "model_design": "Simplify the model by removing the StylometricCNN branch entirely to avoid the index-out-of-bounds error with character IDs, and freeze the BERT backbone to prevent overfitting on the small dataset. This follows the initial plan's strategy of using BERT as a fixed feature extractor with a discriminative learning rate (zero for BERT, high for custom heads). The model will only use BERT [CLS] features and the StylometricMLP, fused via the existing CrossAttentionFusion module. Implementation: comment out the StylometricCNN usage in MultiViewAuthorClassifier forward, replace its contribution with a zero tensor of appropriate size; set requires_grad=False for all BERT parameters. Interface: forward method signature unchanged; char_ids parameter becomes optional but is expected to be passed (can be ignored)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan correctly identifies overfitting as the root cause, suggesting freezing BERT and using a single-phase discriminative learning rate strategy. Historical experiments show that (1) successful approaches kept the transformer frozen and used differential learning rates, while (2) failed experiments that unfroze BERT led to instability and overfitting. Therefore, I focus on two modules: data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add input validation preventing NaN predictions (inspired by a successful historical fix), and model_design to simplify the architecture by removing the CNN branch (which can cause index errors) and freezing BERT entirely, as recommended by the initial plan and historical successes.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add input validation to check for NaN or infinite values in stylometric features and character IDs before feeding them into the model. This prevents gradient explosion and unstable predictions, as seen in a successful historical experiment that fixed NaN issues by reducing learning rates and adding clipping. Implementation: after extracting stylometric features, replace any NaN or inf values with 0; similarly, after tokenizing characters, ensure no out-of-range indices by clamping character IDs to [0, vocab_size-1] and setting padding token to 0. Interface: preserve existing function signatures and data structures.",
    "model_design": "Simplify the model by removing the StylometricCNN branch entirely to avoid the index-out-of-bounds error with character IDs, and freeze the BERT backbone to prevent overfitting on the small dataset. This follows the initial plan's strategy of using BERT as a fixed feature extractor with a discriminative learning rate (zero for BERT, high for custom heads). The model will only use BERT [CLS] features and the StylometricMLP, fused via the existing CrossAttentionFusion module. Implementation: comment out the StylometricCNN usage in MultiViewAuthorClassifier forward, replace its contribution with a zero tensor of appropriate size; set requires_grad=False for all BERT parameters. Interface: forward method signature unchanged; char_ids parameter becomes optional but is expected to be passed (can be ignored)."
