{"reason": "The initial plan calls for a systemic paradigm shift to a fully fine-tuned transformer, but the current execution still uses a frozen DistilBERT with a cross-attention fusion that includes a zero CNN branch, and validation loss stagnates at ~0.7367. Historical experiments show that freezing BERT and removing CNN branches prevents instability and index errors, but the approach remains limited. To break the ceiling, I will fine-tune the transformer backbone in a parameter-efficient way (LoRA) as a middle ground that avoids overfitting while increasing capacity, and simplify the architecture by removing the zero CNN branch entirely, as per the initial plan's direction. The most promising modules for refinement are: model_design to switch to a trainable backbone with LoRA and remove ineffective components; and training_evaluation to adopt a phased learning strategy with gradient clipping and normalization inspired by successful historical experiments.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the frozen DistilBERT with a trainable DeBERTa-v3-base (microsoft/deberta-v3-base) with LoRA adapters (rank=8) to enable parameter-efficient fine-tuning while preventing catastrophic overfitting. Remove the StylometricCNN and its zero tensor contribution, as well as the CrossAttentionFusion module; instead, use the [CLS] token output from the transformer followed by a simple two-layer classification head (dropout+Linear+ReLU+Linear). The stylometric MLP and the 59 features are retained as an additional input to the classifier head via concatenation. This aligns with the initial plan's goal of relying on learned transformer representations while preserving the useful engineered features. The forward method signature will remain unchanged, but char_ids will be ignored.", "training_evaluation": "Implement a phased training schedule: Phase 1 (first 5 epochs) keeps the backbone LoRA adapters frozen and trains only the classification head and stylometric MLP with lr=5e-3; Phase 2 unfreezes LoRA adapters and uses discriminative learning rates (5e-5 for backbone, 5e-3 for head) with AdamW weight decay 0.01, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, gradient normalization to balance contributions, and cosine annealing with warmup. Use label smoothing 0.1 and mixed precision. Early stopping patience=7. This two-phase approach, inspired by successful historical experiments, stabilizes training and prevents overfitting on the small dataset while allowing the transformer to adapt."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan calls for a systemic paradigm shift to a fully fine-tuned transformer, but the current execution still uses a frozen DistilBERT with a cross-attention fusion that includes a zero CNN branch, and validation loss stagnates at ~0.7367. Historical experiments show that freezing BERT and removing CNN branches prevents instability and index errors, but the approach remains limited. To break the ceiling, I will fine-tune the transformer backbone in a parameter-efficient way (LoRA) as a middle ground that avoids overfitting while increasing capacity, and simplify the architecture by removing the zero CNN branch entirely, as per the initial plan's direction. The most promising modules for refinement are: model_design to switch to a trainable backbone with LoRA and remove ineffective components; and training_evaluation to adopt a phased learning strategy with gradient clipping and normalization inspired by successful historical experiments.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the frozen DistilBERT with a trainable DeBERTa-v3-base (microsoft/deberta-v3-base) with LoRA adapters (rank=8) to enable parameter-efficient fine-tuning while preventing catastrophic overfitting. Remove the StylometricCNN and its zero tensor contribution, as well as the CrossAttentionFusion module; instead, use the [CLS] token output from the transformer followed by a simple two-layer classification head (dropout+Linear+ReLU+Linear). The stylometric MLP and the 59 features are retained as an additional input to the classifier head via concatenation. This aligns with the initial plan's goal of relying on learned transformer representations while preserving the useful engineered features. The forward method signature will remain unchanged, but char_ids will be ignored.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a phased training schedule: Phase 1 (first 5 epochs) keeps the backbone LoRA adapters frozen and trains only the classification head and stylometric MLP with lr=5e-3; Phase 2 unfreezes LoRA adapters and uses discriminative learning rates (5e-5 for backbone, 5e-3 for head) with AdamW weight decay 0.01, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, gradient normalization to balance contributions, and cosine annealing with warmup. Use label smoothing 0.1 and mixed precision. Early stopping patience=7. This two-phase approach, inspired by successful historical experiments, stabilizes training and prevents overfitting on the small dataset while allowing the transformer to adapt."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identifies severe overfitting (training loss ~0.07, validation log loss 0.399) and proposes label smoothing and increased weight decay as fixes. Historical successful experiments show that simple regularization (label smoothing, higher weight decay, lower LR, gradient clipping) effectively reduces overfitting. A failed complex plan (multi-phase unfreezing, cross-attention fusion) led to instability, so we avoid that. The right modules to modify are 'training_evaluation' (to implement label smoothing via a custom loss or config, and increase weight decay) and 'model_design' (to ensure the model configuration enables label smoothing if needed, and set correct dropout). Data processing is already adequate.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Keep DeBERTa-v3-small architecture with hidden_dropout_prob=0.1 and attention_probs_dropout_prob=0.1. In the model creation, set label_smoothing=0.1 in the AutoModelForSequenceClassification configuration to enable built-in label smoothing. This directly overcomes overconfidence and aligns with the initial plan. No changes to optimizer parameter groups are needed here; weight decay adjustments are handled in training_evaluation.", "training_evaluation": "Implement the changes: (1) Increase weight_decay from 0.01 to 0.1 for the backbone parameters by setting weight_decay=0.1 in the first group of the optimizer parameter list; keep classifier weight_decay=0.01. (2) The loss function will automatically use label_smoothing=0.1 from model config, no manual cross-entropy needed. (3) Keep cosine schedule with warmup, batch size 8, accumulation steps 4, gradient clipping 1.0, early stopping patience 3, but reduce max epochs to 6 (since early stopping may fire sooner). (4) Ensure no NaN handling changes (current checks suffice). These changes directly target overfitting as confirmed by historical successes."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
